{
  "episodes": [
    {
      "title": "The Whistle in the Night",
      "blurb": "A terrified young woman arrives at Baker Street at dawn, haunted by the mysterious death of her twin sister.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Early in April 1883, Sherlock Holmes woke me at dawn. A visitor waited below in our Baker Street rooms—a young woman dressed in black, shivering with uncontrollable dread."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Helen Stoner",
          "text": "It is not cold that makes me shiver, Mr. Holmes. It is terror."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Her name was Helen Stoner. She lived at Stoke Moran with her violent stepfather, Dr. Grimesby Roylott, an eccentric physician who kept a cheetah and a baboon roaming the grounds. Two years earlier, Helen’s twin sister, Julia, had met a horrifying and inexplicable end."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Helen Stoner",
          "text": "Julia had become engaged to be married. A fortnight before the wedding, she came into my room late at night, asking if I had ever heard a low, clear whistle at three in the morning. I had not. She locked herself in her bedchamber, as we always did to protect against the Doctor’s wild beasts."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "That very night, a howling gale raged outside. Suddenly, a woman’s piercing scream split the darkness."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Helen Stoner",
          "text": "I rushed into the corridor. Julia's door opened, and she appeared, swaying like a drunkard, her face blanched with horror. She collapsed, writhing in terrible convulsions. Before she died in my arms, she pointed toward our stepfather's door and shrieked: 'Oh, Helen! It was the band! The speckled band!'"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "The coroner found no poison, no violence, and the room was locked from within."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "And what brings you to me today, Miss Stoner?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Helen Stoner",
          "text": "I am now engaged myself. And last night, sleeping in Julia’s old room, I heard that same low whistle!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Bent Poker",
      "blurb": "A menacing visit from Dr. Roylott reveals the deadly stakes behind Helen Stoner's plea for help.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Holmes leaned forward, his piercing eyes fixed upon Miss Stoner. He reached out and gently pushed back the black lace fringing her wrist."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "You have been cruelly used."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Five livid bruises—marks of four fingers and a thumb—stood out on her skin. Dr. Roylott’s temper was monstrous. Holmes promised we would visit Stoke Moran that afternoon while the doctor was away. Helen departed, relieved. But barely had the door closed behind her when the sitting-room door was violently hurled back."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "A colossal man framed the doorway, his head brushing the lintel. His face was scorched yellow by the Indian sun and marked with every evil passion."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Grimesby Roylott",
          "text": "Which of you is Holmes?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "My name, sir. Pray take a seat."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Grimesby Roylott",
          "text": "I will not! I traced my stepdaughter here. What has she said to you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "The crocuses promise well this spring."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Grimesby Roylott",
          "text": "Don't toy with me, you busybody! I am a dangerous man to cross!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Roylott seized the steel poker from the hearth and bent it into a curve with his bare hands."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Grimesby Roylott",
          "text": "See that you keep out of my grip!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "He hurled the twisted poker into the grate and strode out. Holmes merely chuckled, took up the steel rod, and with one effort straightened it out again."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "He has nerve and muscle. But Watson, I have examined the mother's will. Roylott loses nearly his entire income if his stepdaughters marry. We must go to Surrey at once—and bring your revolver!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Clues in the Chamber",
      "blurb": "Holmes and Watson inspect Stoke Moran and discover a series of inexplicable alterations in the fatal bedroom.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "By mid-afternoon, Holmes and I had taken a train into Surrey and hurried across the meadows to the crumbling gothic manor of Stoke Moran. Miss Stoner greeted us eagerly. Dr. Roylott was still away in town. Holmes immediately inspected the wing."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Unnecessary repairs on the outer wall forced you into your sister’s room, Miss Stoner. Most convenient. Now let us examine the interior."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "The chamber where Julia died was plain and old. Holmes examined the iron-barred shutters—they were solid and impassable from outside. But then his eyes fixed upon a thick bell-rope hanging beside the bed, its tassel resting upon the pillow. Holmes gave it a tug."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Why, it is a dummy! It is not attached to any bell wire. It is fastened to a hook just above that small ventilator opening into Dr. Roylott's room."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Helen Stoner",
          "text": "How absurd! It was installed only two years ago."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "A ventilator that does not lead outdoors, and a bell-rope that does not ring. And see here, Watson—the bed is clamped firmly to the floor! The occupant could never move it."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Next, Holmes searched Roylott’s adjoining chamber. On an iron safe sat a small saucer of milk. Upon the bedpost hung a dog whip, tied into a running loop. Holmes’s face grew dreadfully grim."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Miss Stoner, you must follow my instructions implicitly. Your life depends on it. When Roylott returns, retire to this room, signal us with a lamp, and slip into your old chamber. Watson and I will spend the night here."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Midnight Vigil",
      "blurb": "Under cover of darkness, Holmes and Watson slip into the sinister manor to await an unknown killer.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "We watched from the village inn as dusk fell. Dr. Roylott returned, his carriage rattling past as his furious bellow echoed through the trees. At eleven o'clock, a single light shone from Helen’s bedroom window—the signal."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Take your pistol, Watson. We must move swiftly."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "We stole through the dark park. Suddenly, a hideous, misshapen figure sprang from the bushes, writhing in the grass before darting into the trees. My heart leaped into my throat."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Quiet, Watson! It is only Roylott’s baboon. But beware—the cheetah also prowls loose."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "We slipped through the bedroom window. Helen vanished to safety, leaving us alone in the dark chamber where her sister had perished. Holmes shut the heavy iron shutters, placed a candle and matches beside him on the bed, and whispered into my ear."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "We must sit in pitch darkness. The least gleam would show through the ventilator. Have your weapon ready, and do not sleep, as you value your life."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "The church bell boomed the passing hours: twelve, one, two, three. Outside, the eerie whine of the cheetah brushed the window. Then, suddenly, a faint glimmer of light pierced the darkness from the ventilator above, followed by the faint smell of burning oil and heated metal."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Someone in the next room had lit a dark lantern. I held my breath, listening into the silence. Then, a low sound broke the still air—a gentle hiss, like escaping steam!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Deadly Sting",
      "blurb": "A sudden terror in the pitch black erupts into a deathly cry that shatters the silence of Stoke Moran.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "The moment that faint, sinister hissing reached our ears, Holmes sprang from the bed. He struck a match and lashed out furiously with his cane at the bell-rope!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "You see it, Watson? Do you see it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "In the brief flare of the match, I heard a low, clear whistle, but the glare blinded me. Holmes’s cane struck the cord again and again, his face white with intense horror and loathing. Then he stopped, staring transfixed at the small ventilator grating."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "For five seconds there was dead silence. Then, through the stillness of the night, there burst the most dreadful cry I have ever heard—a howling shriek of fear, agony, and rage mingling together, echoing across the estate."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Watson",
          "text": "Holmes! What was that?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "It means that it is all over, Watson. Take your revolver. We must enter Roylott’s room."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Holmes lit the lamp and we hurried down the corridor. He pushed open the Doctor's door. On the table stood a dark lantern, shining upon an open iron safe. Beside it sat Dr. Grimesby Roylott in his dressing gown, eyes staring fixedly toward the ceiling in rigid horror."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Bound tightly around his brow was a peculiar brownish-yellow speckled band."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "The band! The speckled band!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "I took a cautious step forward, my gun raised, my blood running cold. As Holmes spoke, the band began to move. From Roylott’s hair reared the flat, diamond-shaped head of a loathsome serpent!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Pit He Dug",
      "blurb": "Holmes unravels the terrifying mechanism of murder and reveals how the deadly plot turned against its master.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "Dr. Roylott sat stone dead, his face frozen in his final agony. The deadly reptile on his brow swayed its swollen neck."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "A swamp adder! The deadliest snake in India. He was dead within ten seconds."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "With lightning swiftness, Holmes seized the looped dog whip from the dead man's lap, dropped the noose over the serpent's neck, and thrust the squirming beast into the iron safe, slamming the door shut."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "narrator",
          "text": "After safely conveying Miss Stoner to her aunt’s home and notifying the police, Holmes and I boarded the train back to London."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Watson",
          "text": "Holmes, the whole monstrous puzzle is clear, yet how did you uncover it?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "At first, Watson, the mention of a band and the gipsies misled me. But when I inspected the room, all became plain. A ventilator opening between two rooms, a dummy bell-rope leading down to a bed bolted in place—the rope was an artificial bridge for a trained climber."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Watson",
          "text": "And the saucer of milk and the whistle?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "Roylott used milk to reward the creature and taught it to return through the ventilator at the sound of the whistle. Every night he slipped the adder through the wall, knowing that sooner or later it would strike the sleeping girl, leaving puncture wounds no ordinary coroner could spot."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Dr. Watson",
          "text": "And when you struck it with your cane..."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Sherlock Holmes",
          "text": "It retreated through the ventilator in a rage and struck the first person it saw: its master! Violence has recoiled upon the violent, Watson, and my conscience is entirely clear."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
